stice one of  the remarkable things about these  documents though is that what they do  first and foremost is protect the  property of the wealthy elites the  prologue to something like this law code  the prologue to Hammurabi's law code  make grand claims to justice to  protecting the weak to protecting the  man of one shekel from the man of one  Mina if you then read on in the text  mostly what it says is don't take  anything that's not yours or the weight  of the state will come down on you very  very forcefully and that's critical to  the operation of Commerce in these  communities in fact we tend to think  based on how we in the modern West view  the relationship between individuals in  the state we tend to think of the state  as being somewhat antithetical to the  operation of private property in fact  history of private property says the  opposite private property wants the  state individuals of property in the  community need the state merchants first  and foremost have to have the state to  enforce the standards on which trade is  based in these early communities now  after a period of unity under for  example this king in his dynasty the  Metis taming world fragmented in the  early second millennium for the last  time into a number of small kingdoms  here's just a colorful example of that  that continued to vie for control of the  region and from this same era we also  begin to get really detailed commercial  records from across mesopotamia not only  in the South but also in the north up  here in what is here the Kingdom of Shem  she odd odd we see in the center of the  city of ash or that area which i think  is brown is that more or less right  something like that in that area that's  the place that later on would develop  into the great Kingdom and Empire of  Assyria but in the beginning of its  history the city of Ashur was a  relatively small city-state at the  center of several really important trade  routes connecting the whole of this  ancient world in fact connecting areas  as far away as modern Iran with the  eastern Mediterranean coastline with  central Turkey the city of a shore in  the early second millennium BC managed a  long-distance trade network that  connected families of merchants in the  city of ashore with their  representatives and family members  stationed all the way up in areas that  are today in central Turkey in order to  manage long-distance trade between these  regions by the way a trade that existed  more or less on the backs of donkeys in  the early second millennium this trade  was so important and so lucrative that  the journey from a short econo Sh meant  that a lot of goods were travelling on  the backs of donkeys and not the people  we know that the people actually walked  because the space on the backs of the  donkeys is too valuable for them so the  people walk next to the donkeys and the  donkeys carried precious material  particularly textiles all the knives  manufactured back down here in southern  Mesopotamia traded up here into a short  and then sent over great distances in to  connect and what came back was quite  often copper and silver  and it made this an extraordinarily  lucrative center of trade and we have  evidence for this from the early second  millennium BC not only from the city of  a shore but also from the city of Mari  here on the Great Bend of the Euphrates  that lay astride really important trade  networks connecting the northern  Euphrates valley with the south and  connecting Mesopotamia with the eastern  Mediterranean coastline in fact there  are thousands of texts from the Royal  archives in the city of Mari that  survived they survived by the way as is  so often the case with our  archaeological material because of  terrible tragedies committed by our  ancient counterparts Hammurabi of  Babylon when he conquered the city of  Maribor down the palace and baked the  Royal archives into permanence so that  we have them those royal archives by the  way are s